Yes. Ubuntu uses APT instead of DNF, and PostgreSQL paths/services differ slightly. Below is the Ubuntu equivalent of your deployment process with explanations and corrections.

1. Install Git
sudo apt update
sudo apt install git -y
2. Install Firewall (UFW)

Ubuntu typically uses UFW instead of firewalld.

sudo apt install ufw -y
sudo ufw enable
sudo ufw status

Allow SSH before enabling if you're connected remotely:

sudo ufw allow ssh
3. Configure Git SSH Access

Generate SSH key:

ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "dinesh.r1@dell.com"

Display public key:

cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub

Add it to your Git server.

Test:

ssh -T git@eos2git.cec.lab.emc.com

Clone repository:

git clone git@eos2git.cec.lab.emc.com:dinesh-r3/qTestSupport-backend.git
cd qTestSupport-backend
4. Create .env
vi .env

Example:

PORT=5001
DB_USERNAME=qtest
DB_PASSWORD=password
DB_PORT=5432
DB_NAME=qtesttoken
DB_HOST=localhost
5. Install Node.js

Recommended (Node 18):

curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_18.x | sudo -E bash -
sudo apt install nodejs -y

Verify:

node -v
npm -v
6. Install PostgreSQL
sudo apt install postgresql postgresql-contrib -y

Start service:

sudo systemctl start postgresql
sudo systemctl enable postgresql

Check status:

sudo systemctl status postgresql
7. Create Database and User

Switch to postgres user:

sudo -i -u postgres

Open PostgreSQL:

psql

Create DB:

CREATE DATABASE qtesttoken;

CREATE USER qtest WITH PASSWORD 'password';

GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON DATABASE qtesttoken TO qtest;

ALTER DATABASE qtesttoken OWNER TO qtest;

Verify:

\l

Connect:

\c qtesttoken

Exit:

\q

Exit postgres account:

exit
8. Configure pg_hba.conf

Find location:

sudo find /etc/postgresql -name pg_hba.conf

Usually:

/etc/postgresql/16/main/pg_hba.conf

(or 15/main, 14/main depending on version)

Edit:

sudo vi /etc/postgresql/*/main/pg_hba.conf

Change:

host    all     all     127.0.0.1/32     peer
host    all     all     ::1/128          peer

or

host    all     all     127.0.0.1/32     ident
host    all     all     ::1/128          ident

to:

host    all     all     127.0.0.1/32     md5
host    all     all     ::1/128          md5

Restart PostgreSQL:

sudo systemctl restart postgresql
9. Test Database Login
psql -U qtest -h localhost -d qtesttoken

You should be prompted for password.

10. Open PostgreSQL Port (Only If Remote Access Needed)

If PostgreSQL and Node are on same server, do not open 5432.

If remote access required:

sudo ufw allow 5432/tcp

Check:

sudo ufw status
11. Install Node Dependencies
npm install --production

or

npm ci --production

for production deployments.

12. Install PM2
sudo npm install -g pm2

Start app:

 
pm2 start src/index.js --name qTest-backend

To delete the app:
pm2 delete qTest-backend

To start the app with opentelemetry instrumentation and load opentelemetry instrumentation first before the application start
pm2 start src/index.js --name qTest-backend --node-args="--import ./instrumentation.mjs"

Check:

pm2 list
pm2 logs qTest-backend

Restart:

pm2 restart qTest-backend
13. Configure PM2 Auto Startup

Generate startup command:

pm2 startup

PM2 will output a command similar to:

sudo env PATH=$PATH:/usr/bin pm2 startup systemd -u ubuntu --hp /home/ubuntu

Run the generated command.

Save process list:

pm2 save
14. Install Nginx
sudo apt install nginx -y

Start:

sudo systemctl start nginx
sudo systemctl enable nginx
15. Create Nginx Reverse Proxy

Create config:

sudo vi /etc/nginx/sites-available/qtest

Add:

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name your-domain.com;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:5001;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;

        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
        proxy_set_header Host $host;

        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    }
}

Enable:

sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/qtest /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/

Remove default site:

sudo rm -f /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/default

Test:

sudo nginx -t

Restart:

sudo systemctl restart nginx
16. Open HTTP/HTTPS Ports
sudo ufw allow 80/tcp
sudo ufw allow 443/tcp

Verify:

sudo ufw status
17. Check Listening Ports

Equivalent commands:

ss -lntp

Check Node:

ss -lntp | grep 5001

Check Nginx:

ss -lntp | grep nginx
18. Install SSL (Let's Encrypt)

Install Certbot:

sudo apt install certbot python3-certbot-nginx -y

Generate certificate:

sudo certbot --nginx -d yourdomain.com

Test renewal:

sudo certbot renew --dry-run
Recommended Production Architecture
Internet/User
      │
      ▼
Nginx (80/443)
      │
      ▼
Node.js (localhost:5001)
      │
      ▼
PostgreSQL (localhost:5432)

In production:

Open only 80 and 443
Keep 5001 private
Keep 5432 private
Access Node and PostgreSQL via localhost only
Let Nginx handle external traffic and SSL

This is the same architecture most enterprise Node.js deployments use on Ubuntu servers.